<p>Iranian human rights activist and 2023 Nobel Peace Prize laureate Narges Mohammadi was transferred from prison to a hospital in Zanjan, northwestern Iran, after her family-run foundation described a catastrophic decline in her health, including two episodes of complete loss of consciousness and a severe cardiac crisis. The foundation said prison doctors concluded her condition could not be treated inside the facility. Mohammadi, 53, won the Nobel Peace Prize for campaigning against the oppression of women in Iran and against the death penalty. Her lawyers previously said she may have suffered a heart attack in late March and appeared weak, underweight and in need of help walking during a later visit. Authorities have detained her since Dec. 12 after she attended a memorial event in Mashhad. In February, a court sentenced her to more than seven years in prison, including six years on charges of “gathering and collusion to commit crimes,” according to her lawyer.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/WTsGu3CsaGZBuxFx2Hv3vCfXj_bPkGEhDslJLo3v6PTrEesT_WLVLhDPAmAO80ECTGGpLLnUnDyRcGkoni7E_BJXLX8mWhuWG99Nm-HzbWVLKsQRgjaexUzkwUJHnP-DvIzCkF6jiH6TwYAcAP-pEoitcSvPX_4TVYN3GimncKPmU2NkNF2QTz8IWMv4fI_-mbs' alt='Narges Mohammadi poses in an undated photo provided by her family.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Treatment status: Reports citing her family said Mohammadi was being treated in an intensive care unit at a Zanjan hospital after the emergency transfer.</li><li>Arrest allegation: Her family alleged she was beaten during her December arrest, saying several men repeatedly struck her head, neck and side; Iranian authorities have not publicly responded to the allegation.</li><li>Earlier sentence: Before her latest detention, Mohammadi had been serving an earlier sentence of more than 13 years on national security-related charges and had been on medical furlough since late 2024.</li><li>Professional background: Mohammadi has also worked as a journalist.</li></ul><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>Narges Mohammadi Foundation: The foundation accused authorities of sustained medical neglect since her latest arrest, according to reports citing its statement. (<a href='https://www.donga.com/news/Inter/article/all/20260502/133850953/1'>Dong-A Ilbo</a>)</li><li>Taghi Rahmani, Mohammadi’s husband: Rahmani, who lives in exile in Paris, said he feared for her life and urged authorities to move her immediately to a specialist hospital. (<a href='https://humenglish.com/latest/nobel-laureate-narges-mohammadi-hospitalised-after-collapsing-in-iranian-prison/'>HUM News English</a>)</li><li>Hamidreza Mohammadi, Mohammadi’s brother: Hamidreza, who lives in Norway, said relatives in Iran were doing everything they could to protect her life, but that the Zanjan prosecutor was blocking their efforts. (<a href='https://www.newsis.com/view/NISX20260502_0003614871'>Newsis</a>)</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='https://abcnews.com/International/wireStory/iranian-nobel-laureate-narges-mohammadi-hospitalized-after-health-132580341'>Iranian Nobel laureate Narges Mohammadi hospitalized after a health crisis in prison</a> - abcnews.com</li><li><a href='https://www.straitstimes.com/world/middle-east/nobel-laureate-mohammadi-in-iran-hospital-after-cardiac-crisis-foundation-says'>Nobel laureate Mohammadi in Iran hospital after ‘cardiac crisis’, foundation says</a> - straitstimes.com</li><li><a href='https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2026/5/1/iranian-nobel-laureate-narges-mohammadi-hospitalised-as-health-deteriorates'>Iranian Nobel laureate Narges Mohammadi hospitalised as health deteriorates</a> - aljazeera.com</li><li><a href='https://www.al-monitor.com/originals/2026/05/nobel-laureate-mohammadi-iran-hospital-after-cardiac-crisis-foundation-says'>Nobel laureate Mohammadi in Iran hospital after &#x27;cardiac crisis&#x27;, foundation says</a> - al-monitor.com</li><li><a href='https://www.nytimes.com/2026/05/01/world/middleeast/narges-mohammadi-iran-hospialized.htm